--Why do enterprise need thousands of servers

Before that, we should understand what is a server is

It is a powerful computer designed to provide the services
Server: The provider ( Finds the request, process it and send the response to the client
Client: The requestor (Browser)

Servers serve: Websites: Html pages, images, videos
Files: Documents, photos, backups
Emails: Sending and receiving messages

Browser request--server- {find, process, send the result}response
There are many servers-Database servers, Messaging servers its not only the powerful device, it serves the services

Can one server handle everything?

No, Traffic spike: Millions of users, if accessing it
It is a powerful computer, but it has its own limits---capacity
Conclusion: relying on a single server is risky for most applications
For large bank—we have lots of apps, internal applications, mobile applications
for that, we need thousands of servers==needed to run all kinds of applications reliably
Challenge: these many servers need massive power and space, so that, not recommended in the office space

These all servers need a house –it is infrastructure---i.e Data Center
Data center: A secure place for hosting servers
Massive scale: Houses hundreds, thousands, or even millions of servers

It is not just a building: Needs specialized infrastructure
Power: uninterrupted power supply
Cooling: make sure not to be over heated
Networking: high speed connectivity
Security: Hosts key business systems

It is more expensive
For startups, they have to buy infra initially, they have to buy more servers in advance, if app goes viral
We don’t know, whether its usage will high or not.
Expensive and high maintenance
Solution is : cloud
Instead buying, we can rent for what we use.

Cloud Key Terminology:

Elasticity: Ability to automatically scale resources up or down based on demand
Agility: Ability to move fast and respond quickly to business needs
Quickly deliver software, quickly provision infra
Global Reach and Geo distribution: The ability to expand to new markets and deploy applications to data center around the world quickly
Availability: Are applications available when users need them
Latency: Reduce the time to respond in b/w client and server
Cloud concepts:
Netflix increases the servers on Friday night, releases the servers on Monday.
For startups, launches new app with minimal cost— pay as you go
A product team provisions infra in minutes to test new features ad releases update faster—Agility
Few applications like health care provides the service even in maintenance—High Availability
A saas company deploys its application in multiple regions to server customers worldwide with low latency -- Global Reach and Geo Distribution
A media platform serves videos from locations closest to users for faster playback – low latency

Buy servers massive for lower cost – Economies for sale

Region: if one datacentre, there are some challenges as if one DC fails
If two Dc’s, in one region- if one region not available
If Dc’s in two different regions, mostly solved, latency (partly solved)
Choosing the Right Region factors:
1.	Compliance!  Be aware about regulations
Why: some countries require citizen data to stay within borders.
2.	Latency: Be close to your users
Goal: Choose a Region closest to the majority of your users.
3.	Services Available: Does the Region support the services you need? New services launch in specific regions first
4.	Pricing- check pricing in different regions
5.	Cost varies b/w regions

Why use multiple Regions?
For low latency: To serve users faster by placing resources closer to them
Global Footprint: Expand services and reach customers worldwide.
Data Residency: Store data in specific geographic locations to meet regulations.
High Availability: Reduce Downtime by running apps in multiple regions
Disaster recovery: protect against regional failures. (ex: natural disasters)

Zone: How to achieve high availability from a single Region.?
Deploying in multiple zones in a region protect against local failures (Power outage, fire)
Solution: Use Zones (Availability Zones)
Zones: Isolated locations within a Region
Each zone has independent power, cooling, n/w connectivity
